<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Llorona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;La Llorona&quot; (&quot;The Weeping Woman&quot;) is a Mexican folk song that takes its name and its shadow from the legend of La Llorona. In the legend she is a ghost, rather like a banshee, who drowned her children, haunts the lover who left her, and weeps for her dead children; to see her foretells death. Folklorists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> have suggested that she is a survival of an old Mesoamerican myth of the earth that eats the dead.</p></div>
